and primary
progressive aphasia.

503
00:25:55,990 --> 00:26:00,050
Most cases are sporadic.
Familial phenotypes

504
00:26:00,050 --> 00:26:03,090
are associated
with the MAPT gene,

505
00:26:03,210 --> 00:26:07,870
GRN gene, and
the C9ORF72 gene.

506
00:26:10,430 --> 00:26:13,870
Frontotemporal dementia
has several clinical

507
00:26:13,870 --> 00:26:15,690
features, which
include behavioral

508
00:26:15,690 --> 00:26:19,310
disinhibition, apathy
or inertia, loss of

509
00:26:19,310 --> 00:26:21,780
sympathy or empathy,
perseverative,

510
00:26:21,780 --> 00:26:24,750
stereotyped, or compulsive
ritualistic behavior,

511
00:26:24,750 --> 00:26:27,870
and hyperorality
and dietary changes.

512
00:26:27,870 --> 00:26:31,270
You can go online
and watch a 60-minute

513
00:26:31,270 --> 00:26:34,870
episode about frontotemporal
dementia, which

514
00:26:34,870 --> 00:26:37,510
is quite interesting
and enlightening.

515
00:26:38,930 --> 00:26:43,030
The diagnosis of
frontotemporal dementia

516
00:26:43,030 --> 00:26:45,410
can be made by
functional neural imaging

517
00:26:45,410 --> 00:26:49,150
with flu-deoxyglucose
PET. There is

518
00:26:49,150 --> 00:26:51,730
no FDA-approved
medication at this time,

519
00:26:51,730 --> 00:26:55,050
and symptoms are
treated with SSRIs,

520
00:26:55,050 --> 00:26:57,430
atypical antipsychotics,
and trazodone

521
00:26:57,430 --> 00:27:00,430
for behavioral
discontrol and agitation.

522
00:27:00,710 --> 00:27:02,190
Cholinesterase inhibitors

523
00:27:02,190 --> 00:27:04,170
do not improve cognition.

524
00:27:05,270 --> 00:27:08,670
So mystery case is
now revealed. This is

525
00:27:08,670 --> 00:27:11,710
fatal familial insomnia.
It's a rare genetic

526
00:27:11,710 --> 00:27:14,350
prion disease, which
has its onset in

527
00:27:14,350 --> 00:27:17,330
the late 40s. It is
severe and progressive

528
00:27:17,330 --> 00:27:20,410
insomnia over months
associated with

529
00:27:20,410 --> 00:27:24,150
dysautonomia, including
tachycardia, hyperhidrosis,

530
00:27:24,150 --> 00:27:27,110
and hyperpyroxia, and
motor and cognitive

531
00:27:27,270 --> 00:27:29,850
symptoms occur late
in the course of

532
00:27:29,850 --> 00:27:32,630
the disease. There's
approximately an 18

533
00:27:32,630 --> 00:27:36,940
-month survival with
this condition. And the

534
00:27:36,940 --> 00:27:40,470
story that this came
from was from this

535
00:27:40,470 --> 00:27:42,390
book, the family that
couldn't sleep, and

536
00:27:42,390 --> 00:27:47,010
it's a Venetian family.
It's also an interesting

537
00:27:47,010 --> 00:27:49,590
read for those that
are interested.

538
00:27:50,010 --> 00:27:53,370
So in terms of
review, it's important

539
00:27:53,370 --> 00:27:55,730
to recognize reversible
causes of memory

540
00:27:55,730 --> 00:27:58,110
loss. It's important
not to miss them.

541
00:27:58,310 --> 00:28:00,550
Understand the underlying
cause of memory

542
00:28:00,550 --> 00:28:02,990
loss based on the
history of the patient.

543
00:28:03,470 --> 00:28:06,110
Thank you very much
for listening, and

544
00:28:06,110 --> 00:28:07,800
this is the end of
this presentation.